{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-117641",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256025/assets/56b1b17826611d0a0b63f24342094d0ef99e9c6c8428829b4a2bdd3593e0caa7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $4$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally,  stretch it parallel to the $x$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ to give $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
